+# Attorney General recipient records for the December 2006 EPA-on-DOT review
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review UTC · B129 / PD02–PD05. The law and Attorney General guidelines in place before the reported December 2006 EPA peer review identify **DOJ's Office of the Attorney General** as an additional place to look for the written results. The period's DOJ correspondence-system notice supplies fields for looking up a receipt or referral. The original peer review, its transmission and its relationship to FAA case **042R0122001** remain unverified. [2002 law, physical pp. 88–89](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-107publ296/pdf/PLAW-107publ296.pdf#page=88) · [2003 guidelines, p. 10](https://www.ignet.gov/sites/default/files/files/agleguidelines.pdf#page=10)
+
+## Which records could exist on each side
+
+| Source | Verified observation and retrieval implication |
+| --- | --- |
+| **Public Law 107-296 § 812(a)**, 116 Stat. **2222–2223**, PDF pp. 88–89 | Added Inspector General Act § 6(e). Paragraph (3) names DOT and EPA among the offices exempt from the initial eligibility determination. Paragraph (7) calls for a collective memorandum of understanding for external review, a copy of that memorandum for the Attorney General and written results for both the reviewed inspector general and the Attorney General. The statutory requirement supplies a recipient-side search target. The executed memorandum, review-specific routing and actual receipts remain missing. [Statute, pp. 88–89](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-107publ296/pdf/PLAW-107publ296.pdf#page=88) |
+| **Attorney General guidelines**, dated **December 8, 2003** on physical p. 11 | Section XII on p. 10 describes periodic reviews of safeguards for law-enforcement powers and communication of results to both recipients. Section XI separately describes a November 1 annual written report. Keep that reporting stream separate from a peer-review transmittal. Section XIII describes the guidelines as internal management guidance and addresses third-party rights. [Sections XI–XIII](https://www.ignet.gov/sites/default/files/files/agleguidelines.pdf#page=10) |
+| **December 2011 CIGIE guide**, printed pp. 8 and 16, physical pp. 12 and 20 | The guide names a **Closed Case Inventory** with case identifiers, dates, referral and disposition fields. It distinguishes the original opinion and observation letters for the reviewed OIG, copies for the Investigations Committee and the opinion letter with attachments for the Attorney General where § 6(e) applies. It describes return of materials obtained from the reviewed office and retention of the final report and supporting appendices by the reviewer. It recommends at least two review cycles for those documents. This is a later version. Actual 2006 forms, distribution and retention must be verified separately. [Inventory, p. 12](https://www.ignet.gov/sites/default/files/files/invprg1211.pdf#page=12) · [Distribution and files, p. 20](https://www.ignet.gov/sites/default/files/files/invprg1211.pdf#page=20) |
+| **2024 EPA-on-DOT review**, physical p. 2, line 36 | The held original names the **DOJ Office of the Attorney General** in its distribution line. This shows a later instance of the proposed recipient, with actual delivery and 2006 practice still unverified. The 2024 case sample concerns a different period. [Distribution, p. 2](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/2024-10/EPA%20JI%20Peer%20Review%20September%202024.pdf#page=2) |
+
+## The correspondence-control route
+
+- The **June 4, 2001 DOJ/003 notice** describes department-wide notice coverage while components continued to operate correspondence tracking systems. Its field examples include control number, organization, document date, receipt date, subject and response status. Original correspondence, routing and enclosures may be present. The **October 30, 2006** notice lists DOJ–003 and its 2001 and 2002 notice history. DOJ's separate description names the Departmental Executive Secretariat as manager of the Attorney General's Correspondence Management System and says scans and responses are present in some folders. A period-matched control record and any attachment manifest would test receipt and scope. [2001 notice, physical pp. 1–3](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2001-06-04/pdf/01-13860.pdf) · [2006 listing, physical p. 1](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2006-10-30/pdf/E6-18009.pdf) · [DOJ system description](https://www.justice.gov/ag/major-information-systems)
+- The **N1-060-94-002** predecessor, item **1a(1)(a)**, describes permanent controlled subject correspondence maintained by the Executive Secretariat, annual cutoff and National Archives transfer **15 years after cutoff**. Its status sheet maps that item to **DAA-0060-2017-0002-0001**. The successor describes permanent controlled correspondence and tracking and index information. It says cutoff is **the end of the official's tenure** and transfer **25 years after cutoff**. Its planned initial transfer span says **2001 to 2005**. These are distinct schedule descriptions, transfer triggers and planning fields. The schedule cross-reference gives a series to check. The actual classification, custody, accession and transfer status of any 2006 peer-review record remain unverified. [Predecessor status and subject files, pp. 1–2](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-justice/rg-0060/n1-060-94-002_sf115.pdf) · [Successor, physical p. 3](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-justice/rg-0060/daa-0060-2017-0002_sf115.pdf#page=3)
+
+## Source differences and scope
+
+- The 2003 scan visibly reads **“6(e)(7)”** on p. 10. Its extracted text at line 29 reads **“G(e)(7)”**. The successor schedule's physical p. 3 shows **“DAA-0060-2017-0002-0001”** on the face and **“DM-0060-201 7-0002-0001”** in the extracted item line. Preserve each layer with its locator.
+- The public successor schedule has **five physical pages** in the acquired PDF and its final footer reads **“Page 5 of 6.”** The publication history and status of any sixth page are unknown. A complete approval packet and transfer inventory could clarify its scope.
+- Eight new official response bodies and the held 2024 comparator were independently hashed. The scoped check covered **31 PDF text anchors**, **three HTML anchors** and the rendered faces of physical pp. 10–11 of the 2003 guidelines and p. 3 of the successor. The PDF reader reported a structure-tree diagnostic while extracting the NARA scan. No material from an unverified missing page or DOJ correspondence folder was used.
+
+## Open records
+
+- [ ] **PD04 / PD05.** Seek the signed § 6(e)(7) memorandum and the version of the PCIE guide used in 2006, the EPA outgoing December 2006 opinion and its exact enclosures, the parallel DOT OIG receiving copy and the Attorney General's **DOJ/003** control entry and receipt. Search by EPA OIG sender, DOT OIG subject, investigative peer-review terms, December 2006 issue date and subsequent receipt dates. The later records system description supplies fields, with actual delivery and indexing still untested.
+- [ ] **PD02–PD05.** Obtain the case universe and sample or supporting checklists to test any link to **042R0122001**, and any distinct DOT response or follow-up. The corrected May 2003 FAA answer and delivery acknowledgment, signed administrative action/no-action determination, and recommendation-specific implementation and closure records are separate acquisitions. Recommendations **1–2** and **3–4** remain separately tracked. [Case-level queue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/OPEN-RECORDS.md)
